2. THE INDEX STEP AND THE LOADER PROMPT (criterion 3). Add the same `rulings` property to INDEX_STEP (~105), with the description 'The step return\'s `rulings` when the index carried them. Empty otherwise.', and add 'rulings' to INDEX_STEP's `required`. Then name the field in the load-state dispatch prompt, in the sentence at ~line 508 that lists what the loader fills from each index step: `fill questions, rulings, needsTests, checks, findingCount, allDirect and reason from that step's `return` object where the index carried them, and leave them empty otherwise.` Without both halves a resumed run reads an index that holds the rulings and drops them on the floor.

3. COLLECTION (criteria 2 and 3). Next to `blockedOnHuman` / `asksTheHuman` (~679) add the run's ruling log:

    const rulings = []
    function carryRulings(label, out) {
      const taken = out && Array.isArray(out.rulings) ? out.rulings.filter(Boolean) : []
      for (let i = rulings.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) if (rulings[i].step === label) rulings.splice(i, 1)
      for (const ruling of taken) {
        rulings.push({ step: label, ruling })
        log(`${label} ruled: ${ruling}`)
      }
      return out
    }

Call it from `step()` (~653) on both of its paths and nowhere else:

    async function step(label, phaseName, run) {
      if (recorded.has(label)) {
        log(`${label}: recorded already, skipping`)
        return carryRulings(label, recorded.get(label))
      }
      phase(phaseName)
      const out = await run()
      recorded.set(label, out)
      return carryRulings(label, out)
    }

Why there: `step()` is the one funnel every recording step passes through — decompose, research, tests, implement, review, replan — live or replayed from the state, so those two call sites are the whole of the collection and `load-state` and `publish`, which are not steps, never reach it. The recorded path is what makes a resumed run's result as rich as an uninterrupted one: the steering projection the state loader returned carries the skipped step's `rulings`, and skipping the dispatch no longer skips the ruling.

Each entry carries the label of the step that made it, which is the whole of the traceability criterion 2 asks for, and entries land in dispatch order, a recovered step landing exactly where a live one would have. Entries already carrying a label are dropped before that label's new ones are pushed: labels are keyed on the increment id, so an increment the planner hands back is worked again under the same labels (see `forgetSteps`, ~666), and without the drop the abandoned attempt's rulings would sit in the result beside the ones that replaced them. A repeated step that rules on nothing therefore clears its earlier rulings, which is the same supersede-with-history semantics the recorder gives a repeated label.

Rejected: collecting at each of the six dispatch sites (six edits, and the resumed path has no dispatch site to edit); a Map keyed by label flattened at the end (same result, one more indirection); stripping `rulings` out of the `recorded` map (they are steering-sized values, and that map is exactly what a skipped step hands on).

4. THE RESULT (criterion 2). Add `rulings` to the object the script returns (~1219), beside `blockedOnHuman`. It is always present, and empty for a run that took none — the same shape `blockedOnHuman` has, so a caller reads both the same way. An empty array in the returned object is not the 'noise' criterion 5 forbids; the heading, the outcome lines and the log lines are, and each of those is conditional.

5. THE PULL-REQUEST BODY (criteria 4 and 5). In `runOutcome()` (~1133), after the `worked` block and before the `blockedOnHuman` block, add:

    if (rulings.length) {
      lines.push('- Rulings this run took on the human\'s behalf:')
      for (const r of rulings) lines.push(`  - ${r.step}: ${r.ruling}`)
    }

And in the publish prompt (~1171), inside step 3's body paragraph, between the sentence ending `so its unmerged work is findable without being in the diff.` and `End the body with a blank line,`, insert:

    (rulings.length
      ? 'The outcome below lists the rulings this run took on the human\'s behalf: give them a ' +
        '`## Rulings` heading of their own in the body, one bullet each, naming the step that ' +
        'made the ruling. '
      : '') +

`## Rulings` is the heading text, named verbatim in the prompt so the body is predictable rather than left to the agent's taste. A run with no rulings adds nothing: no outcome lines, no instruction, no heading asked for and none built — that is criterion 5, and it falls out of both blocks being conditional on the same array. The publishing agent still reads nothing: the rulings arrive inside the outcome the script already hands it, and the standing `Do NOT open <dir>/backlog.json` line is untouched.

6. WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT DONE. (a) No agent page and no brief edit. The researcher page's line 'These two are also your structured return' names which of its recorded fields the caller triages on; `questions` and `summary` already sit in that schema beside them, so a fourth field contradicts nothing, and the brief already tells every agent the schema names what the structured object carries. (b) Rulings of an increment an earlier session closed are not recovered. `close` moves an increment's steps into its `attempts` and the index projects `attempts` as a count only, so those returns are not in what the state loader can see at all; recovering them would mean changing the index's projection, which is a change to the recorder every run pays for, for history the result already summarises in the closed increment's note. Criterion 3 is about the steps a resumed run skips — the ones in `recorded` — and those are covered in full. (c) No `rulings` in PUSH or in STATE's top level, for the reason in step 1.

The reviewer filed one finding and it is the whole of this round: a run resumed after an earlier session closed an increment loses that increment's rulings. `close` in skills/agent-brief/assets/backlog.mjs moves an increment's steps (and the run-level `replan:<id>` with them) into `attempts` and empties `steps`; `index` then prints only `attempts: <count>`. The state loader is asked for `increments[].steps` alone, so those rulings reach neither `recorded` nor `carryRulings`, and the increment loop never enters a `done` increment. Result: the reviewer's probe saw 11 rulings from an uninterrupted two-increment run and 6 from the same work resumed after i1 had closed.

Fix it where the loss happens — in the index — and seed the workflow's ledger from what the loader brings back. Three edits, in this order.

1) skills/agent-brief/assets/backlog.mjs, `index`. Add one field to each increment's projection, beside `attempts`, and a helper above `index` next to `steering`:

```
// A close archives an attempt's steps, and nothing in that archive is steering
// any more — except a ruling, which is a decision taken on the human's behalf
// and has to reach the result of the run that resumes after the close. So the
// index carries the archived steps' rulings and nothing else out of `attempts`,
// through the same projection the current steps take: a ruling too long to be a
// steering value is content, and content is read from the file.
function attemptRulings(increment) {
  const out = []
  for (const attempt of increment.attempts || []) {
    for (const step of attempt.steps || []) {
      const projected = steering(step && step.return).rulings
      const kept = Array.isArray(projected) ? projected.filter(Boolean) : []
      if (kept.length) out.push({ label: (step && step.label) || '', rulings: kept })
    }
  }
  return out
}
```

and in the increments map of `index`, directly after `attempts: (increment.attempts || []).length,`:

```
      attemptRulings: attemptRulings(increment),
```

The field is always present, `[]` when the archive ruled nothing, exactly as `steps: []` is. Order is the order `close` archived in: the increment's own steps first, then the run-level steps it carried. Change nothing else in `close`, `steps`, `record` or the file format — the projection is derived from what is already stored, so a state file written before this change indexes its archived rulings too.

2) workflows/agile-loop.js, the `STATE` schema. In the `increments` item, add the property

```
          attemptRulings: {
            type: 'array',
            description:
              'The index\u2019s `attemptRulings` for this increment: the rulings of the steps its closed attempts archived, each with the label of the step that made them. Empty when the index carries none.',
            items: {
              type: 'object',
              properties: {
                label: { type: 'string' },
                rulings: { type: 'array', items: { type: 'string' } },
              },
              required: ['label', 'rulings'],
              additionalProperties: false,
            },
          },
```

and add `'attemptRulings'` to that item's `required` list. Write the description with a plain ASCII apostrophe the way the neighbouring descriptions do; the snippet above only escapes it to survive this JSON.

3) workflows/agile-loop.js, the state-loader prompt (the `agent(...)` call at the `Load state` dispatch). After the sentence that ends `...and leave them empty otherwise.`, add one sentence: each increment also carries the index's `attemptRulings`, returned as it stands and empty where the index has none. Keep the existing ban on reading the file itself.

4) workflows/agile-loop.js, the seeding. Immediately after the `carryRulings` function definition and before `const cutWasReplayed`, add:

```
// The rulings of every increment an earlier session closed. `close` archived
// their steps and the loop never enters a closed increment, so this is the only
// place they can be recovered, and it runs before any step does — a resumed
// run's result is then as rich as an uninterrupted one's. They go through
// `carryRulings` for its label rule: an increment worked again this session
// replaces its own earlier attempt's entries as its steps record.
if (savedIndex) {
  for (const increment of savedIndex.increments || []) {
    for (const archived of increment.attemptRulings || []) {
      carryRulings(archived.label, { rulings: archived.rulings })
    }
  }
}
```

The placement is load-bearing: `const rulings` is declared at the top of `carryRulings`'s block, so any earlier call is a temporal-dead-zone error. Do not add a second log line for recovered rulings — `carryRulings` already logs one per ruling, and a run whose archive ruled nothing logs nothing, which is what criterion 5 asks for.

Nothing else changes. `runOutcome()`, the publish prompt, the per-role schemas and the `rulings` field on the result are as the previous round left them and already satisfy criteria 1, 2, 4 and 5; the seeded entries flow into all of them because they sit in the same `rulings` array.

Decisions, with what was rejected:
- Derive the archived rulings in `index` rather than roll them up into a new durable field written by `close`. A roll-up would only cover closes made after the change, so every state file already on disk would stay poor; the derived projection reads the archive that is already there.
- Carry only the rulings out of `attempts`, not the archived steps' full steering projection. The index's contract is that it does not grow with the run; rulings are rare and short, an archive of whole steps is not.
- Seed every archived attempt, whatever status it closed with, rather than only the accepted ones. That is what an uninterrupted run does — it collected those rulings live — and `carryRulings`'s per-label replacement already drops an abandoned attempt's entry as soon as the same label records again.
- Reuse `steering()` for the projection instead of a bespoke size rule, so one rule decides what the index carries; an over-long ruling is dropped there exactly as an over-long question is.
- Keep `attemptRulings` present and empty rather than omitted when there is none: the schema requires it, and an always-present empty list is the shape `steps` and `criteria` already have. Criterion 5 is about the human-facing result, the publish prompt and the chat, none of which gains anything from an empty list in the index.
- No root `CLAUDE.md` for `test-repo.sh`: a memory file at the repository root would load into every session here and be inherited by every subagent, which `.claude/rules/agents.md` forbids for exactly this reason. `test-repo.sh`'s conventions stay in its own comments.
